e rich so  this requires a partnership between  government and industry to make sure  that britain make gets the best  advantage out of it and also to make  sure it's done in a fair way if you  model the cost of the rising interest  rates on this  look we always have an eye on everything  but green guilts are already part of the  policy mix we want to use them to make  sure we get this transition done in a  fair way okay okay um now i just want to  have a look at a piece that you wrote  for the times uh back in march 2020. now  this was at the time of the leadership  contest uh to replace jeremy corbyn and  this is what you said if it's quote  uh okay that is not the quote  i was hoping for so the quote said after  labour lost its third election in a row  in 1987 neil kinnick launched a no holds  barred policy review in which everything  was on the table including the sacred  cows such as support for unilateral  nuclear disarmament he did that because  he knew that fundamental change was  necessary if labour was to win again and  then he went on to say after a  shattering defeat the key question is  direction it means having a policy  platform that looks to the future  i just wonder if you feel like that you  know genuine no holds barred policy  review has happened under kirstalma  well i've always been clear that  leadership wasn't just about a change of  personnel it had to be a change of  direction to  when you lost  four elections in a row  uh you can't just go back with the same  policy platform and  a person in a different suit and expect  the electorate to  uh to take a different attitude so i'm  very much in favor of root and branch  examination of things but if you take  the greek and that's happened if you  change to take the example we've been  discussing this morning on the green  agenda there i think you've got an  ambitious program  for a transition that's going to happen  anyway but i want us to be hungry for  change in every area in education policy 